nd then went into business and is  now a major figure with mci of all  things so he's led a very interesting  life where are you from i was born in  Grand Rapids Minnesota not Michigan I  often will have people call me and say  there's a typographical error on your  resume but Grand Rapids Minnesota which  was the birthplace of Francis gum later  Judy Garland that's what it's known for  in northern Minnesota and I moved around  a lot as a kid my father  there was a traveling salesman and we  took it all seriously he was from Canada  we lived in Canada for some years but I  basically grew up in the Twin Cities of  minnesota went to the University of  Minnesota and then went on to ann arbor  university michigan for graduate school  what year did you get out of the  University of 1967 how long have you  been in Washington totally well I came  to Washington after two years in  graduate school at Michigan in 1969 and  worked as a congressional fellow after a  gene iden berg had taught turned me on  to Washington from his experience as a  congressional fellow I set that as a  goal I came here and spent the year I  went back to Ann Arbor and finished my  dissertation my PhD dissertation then I  went off to teach in Italy at Johns  Hopkins School of Advanced International  Studies in Bologna for a year and came  back here in 1972 to teach at Catholic  University and basically except for a  year in Stanford at the Center for  Advanced Study and behavioral sciences  I've been here ever since what's a  political scientist a very good question  probably a technical terms that somebody  with a PhD in political science but it's  really I would define a political  scientist as somebody who tries to look  at political events and political  patterns and draw some perspectives some  generalizations some broad framework  some theory out of them you don't have  to be an academic necessarily to do that  but what would distinguish a political  scientist from say simply the myriad of  pol