ry active in that  area and that means not just people into  elective office but into a point of  office or even into service of another  form I'm very concerned that we've  created all kinds of barriers a real  formal and informal to good people even  being encouraged to spend time in those  things I crusade to get them out there  but the reality at this point is when I  look at the climate generally and the  barriers that we've created I wouldn't  do it right now and I'd have a hard time  encouraging somebody else to do it right  now that de-stresses me but it keeps me  from considering any kind of bid for  office if someone were to follow you  around on most days what would a day be  like there isn't a real typical day  Brian but I suppose if you try to meld  together a week and then draw a typical  day out of it there'd be a couple of  hours on the  mostly with journalists I spent a lot of  time with journalists and I've been  caricatured in the past for all of the  time that I spend with journalists and  the quotes that are used but I find that  it's very useful I try to do in part  frankly because I think journalists  these days especially often have no  sense of historical perspective or  context and the stories that they write  and I really try and help provide some  of that you get people coming on to a  beat who just don't know what's been  done before or where this fits in and  that's required and I'll often spend  time with journalists showing them  things to read or trying to give them a  little bit of an education we're from  your office at AEI yeah mostly for my  own go there every day that's my basin  on there when I'm in town that's where I  go in the morning and i also find  frankly that when i talk to journalists  there's a feedback there too i learn an  awful lot because there are people out  there on the ground I'm up on Capitol  Hill on an average day once or twice a  day I may be giving a talk I might be  having lunch with somebody with one of